  "result": "Not quite. Pipelining doesn't make any single instruction faster, and 4 stages doesn't give you a quarter of the time.\n\nSay each instruction takes 4 cycles (one per stage), and you run 5 instructions:\n\n- **No pipeline:** 5 × 4 = 20 cycles.\n- **4-stage pipeline:** 4 cycles for the first instruction to get through, then one more finishes every cycle after that. That's 4 + 4 = 8 cycles.\n\nThe first instruction still takes 4 cycles, so it isn't any faster. What changed is that a new instruction finishes every cycle, so throughput went up. 20 → 8 is about 2.5× faster, not 4×.\n\nYou only approach 4× with a very long program, because the 3 cycles spent filling the pipeline get spread thin. Stalls from hazards then pull you back down. That's why adding stages isn't automatically worth it when you design a CPU.\n\nQuick check: with the same 4 stages, how many cycles would 100 instructions take, and roughly what speedup is that?",
